@{
    ViewBag.Title = "View Rules";
}

@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}

<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ul class="rules unordered">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first"><span class="rule-item">OW13000 </span> PUT HTML in Views and Partial Views (not in a controller) <span class="lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                The strength of view pattern is the readability of view template files and the partial view
                is a powerful extensibility and reuse mechanism. For example, we can include the same view in an
                admin view, without writing another line of code.
            </p>
            <p>The following example shows us how to call a partial view:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    &#64;{   Html.RenderPartial("~/Views/Report/CurrencyWinLoss/Main.Header.cshtml", Model); }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW13001 </span> ENABLE (auto-generated) client side validation <span class="lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                Previously, web developers were faced with the dilemma of keeping client and server validation synchronized.
                Starting with ASP.NET MVC 2, it has become easy to add client side validation. Here is the example:
            </p>
            <p>First, we need to enable client side validation in web.config:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: xml">
                    <appsettings>
                        &lt;add key="ClientValidationEnabled" value="true" /&gt;
                        &lt;add key="UnobtrusiveJavaScriptEnabled" value="true" /&gt;
                    </appsettings>
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>And here is out Login form:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    &#64;using (Html.BeginForm())
                    {
                        <div class="form-row">
                            &#64;Html.LabelFor(m => m.UserName)
                            &#64;Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.UserName, new { &#64;class = "inputtext" })
                            &#64;Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.UserName, "<< " + ViewResources.required, new { id = "valMsgUserName" })
                        </div>
                        Html.ValidationSummary(true, ViewResources.LoginUnsuccessMsg, new { id = "msgBox" });
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>
                Now if we try to edit a form's field which does not match the data annotation, the client side
                validation will fail and immediate feedback is given to the user.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW13002 </span> INSERT server-side comments in templates <span class="lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                User server side comments in the view templates for commenting. These comments are stripped out
                before the server returns the HTML representation.
            </p>
            <p>The following line demonstrates a server side comment:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: html">
                    <%-- This is a server side template comment --%> 
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>
                Do not use HTML comments in the view template because they will be rendered to the web browser
                and could be viewed by an advanced (and potentially malicious) user.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW13003 </span> USE HTMLHelper extension methods <span class="lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>The System.Web.Mvc.Html class contains useful HTML extension methods. These extension methods include helpers for:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Form generation (BeginForm).</li>
                <li>Input filed generation (chechbox, hidden, radio button, textbox).</li>
                <li>Link generation (ActionLink).</li>
                <li>XSS Protection (Encode).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                These HTML Helpers should be utilized as much as possible. For example, the following code creates a link
                using the route table back to the default action on the controller from which the view is called.
                Here is the example:
            </p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    &#64;using (Html.BeginForm())
                    {
                        Html.Hidden("Hidden", "Hidden");
                        Html.ActionLink("Home", "Index", "Home");
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
